**The options**

| | `doctor nlp` | `doctor tapped` (deleted) | breaks callers |
|---|---|---|---|
| A keep | filter ✓ | hello-world report, env rows ∅ ✗ | no |
| B refuse slug-shaped token | refused ✗ | error ✓ | yes |
| C refuse only when both come up empty | filter ✓ | error ✓ | no |
| D filter moves to a flag | `--filter=nlp` | error ✓ | yes |

B, as the entry words it ("refuse an unmatched exact-looking token"), has no clean test. `nlp` and `tapped` look the same, both bare lowercase words. Any rule for "looks like a slug" would reject real filters.

C tells them apart by what each lookup finds. The token misses the record and narrows the env to zero rows, so it's neither an instance nor a useful filter:

```js
// systems/ghost/trajectories/instance/doctor.js:37 — proposed
export async function doctor(ctx) {
  const found = await split(ctx);
  if (found.error || found.aborted) return (ctx.effect = found);
  const { target, filter } = found;
  if (target) paladin.env.set("VIVA_INSTANCE_MOUNT", target, "flag");

  await lifecycle.mount(paladin.instance);
  const mount = paladin.scope.instance.absolute;
  const instance = (await paladin.ledger.instances.lookup(mount))?.slug ?? basename(mount);

  const rows = narrow(paladin.check.environment(paladin.instance), filter);
  if (filter && !rows.length) {
    return (ctx.effect = { error: `no instance '${filter}' and no env row matches it on ${instance} — viva instances/list` });
  }
```

It's one guard, the error names both readings, and `split()` stays as it is.

**The choice:** do you want `doctor <one>` to stay overloaded and refuse only when both readings miss (C, my recommendation), or should the positional always mean an instance, with filtering moved to `--filter=` (D, cleaner grammar but it breaks `doctor nlp`)?

