**instance/doctor: what `tapped` means with one argument**

**Context.** The ledger entry puts the cause in `locate()`, but that's the wrong file. `locate()` already refuses an unknown name: `target.js:22` returns `no instance 'tapped'`. It only runs when doctor gets two arguments. With one argument, `split` in `doctor.js` never calls `locate()`. It tries the record, and if that throws, it quietly treats the word as a search filter over the environment rows of the instance you're already on:

```js
// systems/ghost/trajectories/instance/doctor.js:23
// `doctor <target> <filter>` is unambiguous; `doctor <one>` is not. one param names the instance
// only if the record resolves it — otherwise it narrows the instance you are on.
  return await paladin.ledger.instances
    .resolve(first)
    .then((held) => ({ target: held.mount }), () => ({ filter: first }));
```

After `instance/delete tapped`, `resolve("tapped")` throws `no record 'tapped'`. The `() => ({ filter: first })` fallback catches that and turns it into a filter. Doctor then mounts the session instance (hello-world) and prints its full report with the env rows narrowed to nothing. That fallback swallows the "no instance" error.

**The choice.** One lowercase word could be an instance slug or a search like `doctor nlp`. The comment at `doctor.js:8` names `nlp` as a normal filter. Slugs are lowercase too, so you can't tell the two apart by how the word looks. That rules out the "refuse an exact-looking token" option in the ledger, because it would break `doctor nlp`.

What decides it: when neither reading matches anything.

| option | `doctor nlp` | `doctor tapped` (deleted) | `doctor hello` (prefix) |
|---|---|---|---|
| A — keep as is | narrows ✓ | hello-world report ✗ | filter, not the instance |
| B — refuse anything that looks like a slug | error ✗ | error ✓ | error |
| C — refuse only when both readings are empty | narrows ✓ | `no instance 'tapped'` ✓ | filter, not the instance |

