**registry predicate: name candidates**

The check already exists. `Pensieve.register` makes it right before it throws, and calls the case "shadow":

```js
// subsystems/paladin/prototypes/pensieve.js
    const held = slugMap.get(version);
    if (held && held.source?.absolute !== module.source?.absolute)
      throw new Error(`… already registered from ${held.source?.absolute} — a second declaration at ${module.source?.absolute} would shadow it`);
```

So the new method is that same condition as a boolean, placed next to `has(reference)`. Identity is `owner/type/slug@version` and the path is `source`. Each candidate below is used as `registry.X(source, module)`:

| name | reads as | collision |
|---|---|---|
| `shadows` | `if (await registry.shadows(source, module))` | none. Pensieve's own word, one line up |
| `elsewhere` | `registry.elsewhere(source, module)` | none |
| `twinned` | `registry.twinned(source, module)` | none |
| `displaces` | `registry.displaces(source, module)` | none |
| `claimed` | — | ✗ paladin `claim` already exists (evicts `observe`) |
| `held` | — | ✗ used as a local in `reconcile` and `pensieve.register` |

**Pick: `shadows`.** Pensieve already names this failure "would shadow it", so the predicate and the throw share one word. It also reads correctly: the module at `source` shadows the copy already held. With `elsewhere` the sentence works, but it describes a location, not the conflict.

The name also decides what the body does. It should reuse the pensieve lookup rather than read `references()`, because the record stores package roots, not modules:

```js
// subsystems/paladin/prototypes/ledger/registry.js
  shadows(source, module) {
    const { owner, type, slug, version } = module.manifest;
    const held = this.pensieve.get(owner)?.get(type)?.get(slug)?.get(version);
    return Boolean(held) && held.source.absolute !== source.absolute;
  }
```

It's synchronous: the pensieve lookup is in memory, so there's nothing to await, unlike `has`, which reads the record from disk. Nothing is written until you give the name and a go.

**Inspector: effect no longer triggers itself**

cause = `commons/instances/hello-world/app/Inspector.svelte:5`. The effect reads `held` (for `held?.seen`) and then writes `held`. Because it reads `held`, the effect depends on it, so each write runs it again. That loops forever and Svelte stops it with `effect_update_depth_exceeded` on mount.

fix = wrap the read of the previous `seen` in `untrack`, so the effect depends only on `row`:

```diff
// commons/instances/hello-world/app/Inspector.svelte
 <script>
+  import { untrack } from "svelte";
   let { row } = $props();
   let held = $state(null);
   $effect(() => {
-    held = { ...row, seen: (held?.seen ?? 0) + 1 };
+    held = { ...row, seen: untrack(() => held?.seen ?? 0) + 1 };
   });
 </script>
```

`seen` now goes up once per `row` change, which is what the original code meant to do.

**Verified:** it compiles clean with the Svelte 5 compiler (`ok`, no warnings). I have not mounted it in a browser. `runtime/watch` deploys every save under `commons`, so the change is already live. Check it with a mount in anima `:1794`.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- In Svelte 5 an `$effect` depends on every `$state` it reads, including a read on the right side of its own assignment. Reading and writing the same state in one effect loops unless the read goes through `untrack`.
- `$derived` can't do this job. `seen` depends on its own previous value, and a derived can't read itself.
